    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "inventarch 1.0.0: the public reset changes the licence, the repository, the kit set (private kits removed, two authoring kit ids retired), the release format and the hooks' behaviour, so it is a breaking release under a fresh history."
    rationale "A repository bound to a kit the public core no longer carries must notice the change; a major version says so."

@decision public-plugin-ia
  meaning
    says "The plugin is ia (ia@inventarch; the catalogue stays inventarch, and kit ids do not change). Its model-invocable entry skills are the three lanes, ia:work, ia:knowledge and ia:delivery; the setup skill is the user-invoked ia:init, merged with the CLI-rendered plugin's init steps, beside a user-invoked ia:doctor; its agents are ia:reader, ia:checker and ia:worker; a capability bound to a lane is shown as ia-<lane>:<activity>, the binding key re-spelled, whichever kit supplies it (a lane's entry, <lane>.*, reads ia-<lane>). Until the CLI changes so that ia host adds this marketplace and installs ia@inventarch from it, users remove the CLI-rendered registration of the same catalogue name (claude plugin marketplace remove inventarch) before adding this one."
  work
    title "Name the one public plugin ia, with three lanes"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What is the public plugin called, and which skills does it advertise?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "The plugin is ia (ia@inventarch; the catalogue stays inventarch, and kit ids do not change). Its model-invocable entry skills are the three lanes, ia:work, ia:knowledge and ia:delivery; the setup skill is the user-invoked ia:init, merged with the CLI-rendered plugin's init steps, beside a user-invoked ia:doctor; its agents are ia:reader, ia:checker and ia:worker; a capability bound to a lane is shown as ia-<lane>:<activity>, the binding key re-spelled, whichever kit supplies it (a lane's entry, <lane>.*, reads ia-<lane>). Until the CLI changes so that ia host adds this marketplace and installs ia@inventarch from it, users remove the CLI-rendered registration of the same catalogue name (claude plugin marketplace remove inventarch) before adding this one."
    rationale "Everything a person types or reads starts with ia, and one plugin with three lanes is what the public offering is."

@decision public-door-mcp
  meaning
    says "The core ships one MCP server, the IA door, as plugins/ia/.mcp.json: server ia, run as node scripts/door.mjs, a launcher that starts npx -y @inventarch/mcp-door at the plugin's IA pin (lib/cli.mjs PINNED, set by the current CLI pin decision) never through a shell (on Windows, where npx is a .cmd shim, it runs npm's npx-cli.js with node), with stdio passed through and IA_KITS_DIR set to the plugin's config/kits. Codex reads a plugin's .mcp.json too, so the Codex manifest keeps the door off with an empty mcpServers until it is qualified there. Claude Code names its tools mcp__plugin_ia_ia__<tool>, and the reader, checker and worker allowlists name ia_session, ia_catalogue and ia_method that way (reader and worker also ia_position, ia_read and ia_vocabulary). The lanes call ia_session once per session, ia_catalogue {lane} to choose a key and ia_method {key} for the packet; the lanes use only the door's tools, with no script fallback (task drop-door-fallback, done). tools/validate.py admits that one file, exactly."
  work
    title "Ship the IA door as the core's MCP server"
    status superseded
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the core reach the method catalogue and packets without a per-workspace registration?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "The core ships one MCP server, the IA door, as plugins/ia/.mcp.json: server ia, run as node scripts/door.mjs, a launcher that starts npx -y @inventarch/mcp-door at the plugin's IA pin (lib/cli.mjs PINNED, set by the current CLI pin decision) never through a shell (on Windows, where npx is a .cmd shim, it runs npm's npx-cli.js with node), with stdio passed through and IA_KITS_DIR set to the plugin's config/kits. Codex reads a plugin's .mcp.json too, so the Codex manifest keeps the door off with an empty mcpServers until it is qualified there. Claude Code names its tools mcp__plugin_ia_ia__<tool>, and the reader, checker and worker allowlists name ia_session, ia_catalogue and ia_method that way (reader and worker also ia_position, ia_read and ia_vocabulary). The lanes call ia_session once per session, ia_catalogue {lane} to choose a key and ia_method {key} for the packet; the lanes use only the door's tools, with no script fallback (task drop-door-fallback, done). tools/validate.py admits that one file, exactly."
    rationale "One door carries every method for every host; launching it from the plugin needs no local path and no second server, and pinning it like the CLI keeps it reviewable."

@decision public-kit-handlers
  meaning
    says "A kit may ship handlers.json (format 1: session handlers with their events, prompt, tool with phase pre or post and a tool-name match, and stop, each a .mjs script inside the kit with a timeout of at most 25 seconds). scripts/handlers.mjs runs them from SessionStart (every source), UserPromptSubmit, PreToolUse and PostToolUse on edits and shell commands, and Stop, only for installed kits (this plugin's config/kits and <IA home>/kits) whose folder holds the script, so a fetched release, which carries no executables, never runs one. Each runs as node <script> with the hook JSON on stdin and IA_KIT_ROOT, IA_WORKSPACE_ROOT and IA_HOME set; the core merges their additionalContext at most 2,000 characters per kit; a pre block is honoured only in a workspace with .ia/governance.json; with no handler declared every hook is a silent no-op. onwrite.json runs as tool post handlers. handlers.json is a sidecar the sources digest covers, and its scripts must be declared assets. A run may also be the one form ${PLUGIN}/scripts/method-advise.mjs, the core's own allowlisted script, for a kit that ships no code (decision public-method-advisory-run-points)."
  work
    title "Run the handlers installed kits declare"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does a kit run session, prompt, tool and stop logic, such as a governance ledger, without a plugin of its own?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A kit may ship handlers.json (format 1: session handlers with their events, prompt, tool with phase pre or post and a tool-name match, and stop, each a .mjs script inside the kit with a timeout of at most 25 seconds). scripts/handlers.mjs runs them from SessionStart (every source), UserPromptSubmit, PreToolUse and PostToolUse on edits and shell commands, and Stop, only for installed kits (this plugin's config/kits and <IA home>/kits) whose folder holds the script, so a fetched release, which carries no executables, never runs one. Each runs as node <script> with the hook JSON on stdin and IA_KIT_ROOT, IA_WORKSPACE_ROOT and IA_HOME set; the core merges their additionalContext at most 2,000 characters per kit; a pre block is honoured only in a workspace with .ia/governance.json; with no handler declared every hook is a silent no-op. onwrite.json runs as tool post handlers. handlers.json is a sidecar the sources digest covers, and its scripts must be declared assets. A run may also be the one form ${PLUGIN}/scripts/method-advise.mjs, the core's own allowlisted script, for a kit that ships no code (decision public-method-advisory-run-points)."
    rationale "Governance, Studio and work engines become kit scripts, so the public core runs them and no private plugin is needed; opting in per workspace keeps a kit from blocking work nobody asked it to govern."

@decision public-kit-dependencies
  meaning
    says "A kit release's .ia/release.json dependencies other than inventarch/language name a kit id and range. Adoption (kit.mjs add), add --from and the restored read root resolve each dependency, transitively and dependencies first, from the kits this plugin carries, then <IA home>/kits, then the dependency's own folder next to the kit; the first whose latest release satisfies the range wins. Its sources must pack to that recorded release, and its archive goes with the language base into the copy's or the workspace's vendor catalog, so ia install --catalog and ia restore --catalog resolve it. A missing dependency, a cycle or two releases of one kit is refused before anything is written."
  work
    title "Vendor the kits a kit depends on"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does a kit that requires another kit's system install and restore?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A kit release's .ia/release.json dependencies other than inventarch/language name a kit id and range. Adoption (kit.mjs add), add --from and the restored read root resolve each dependency, transitively and dependencies first, from the kits this plugin carries, then <IA home>/kits, then the dependency's own folder next to the kit; the first whose latest release satisfies the range wins. Its sources must pack to that recorded release, and its archive goes with the language base into the copy's or the workspace's vendor catalog, so ia install --catalog and ia restore --catalog resolve it. A missing dependency, a cycle or two releases of one kit is refused before anything is written."
    rationale "A method kit that mints no words requires the vocabulary kit that does; adoption has to bring that kit along, or the native install refuses."

@decision public-delegate
  meaning
    says "Only through the user-invoked ia:delegate (disable-model-invocation), off by default until the person sets delegation true in ~/.ia/config.json or IA_DELEGATION=1. scripts/delegate.mjs builds a codex exec or claude -p command that gets the IA door (--mcp-config for claude -p, -c mcp_servers.ia for codex exec) and the brief with the packet inline on stdin, sets IA_DELEGATION_DEPTH one higher, refuses when it is already 1 or more, applies a timeout (900 seconds by default, at most 3,600) and runs only with --run."
  work
    title "Delegate across models only on the person's word"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-30"
    ended "2026-10-30"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "May the core hand a step to another model's command line?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "Only through the user-invoked ia:delegate (disable-model-invocation), off by default until the person sets delegation true in ~/.ia/config.json or IA_DELEGATION=1. scripts/delegate.mjs builds a codex exec or claude -p command that gets the IA door (--mcp-config for claude -p, -c mcp_servers.ia for codex exec) and the brief with the packet inline on stdin, sets IA_DELEGATION_DEPTH one higher, refuses when it is already 1 or more, applies a timeout (900 seconds by default, at most 3,600) and runs only with --run."
    rationale "Cross-model delegation spends another account and reaches outside the session, so the person turns it on and starts it, and one level deep keeps it from fanning out."

@decision public-kit-trust
  meaning
    says "A kit may run code (a handlers.json run script, an onwrite.json command or a checks.json command) only when its publisher, the kit id's namespace, with which a .ia/release.json publisher field must agree, is on the plugin's config/trusted-publishers.json, which ships as inventarch alone, and its sources digest equals its latest release's recorded sources, with .ia/release.json naming that release; a handler's run script must also be a declared asset, except the core's own allowlisted ${PLUGIN} script (decision public-method-advisory-run-points), whose sidecar the digest covers. Any other kit, and a kit with no recorded release, is data only: a handler entry may carry a context text, delivered as additionalContext with no process; its run entries and onwrite.json commands are skipped, its checks are refused with IA-CORE-KIT-UNTRUSTED, and the SessionStart answer names each such kit once, with why. A pre handler of a kit whose publisher is not on the list never blocks. In a governed workspace a trusted pre handler that fails, times out or answers past the output cap refuses the call, and an altered gate refuses every pre-tool call: while a kit whose id names a trusted publisher declares a pre handler whose code the trust check refuses (its bytes are not its recorded release, or its script is not a declared asset), each such call is refused, naming the kit and the fix, which is to reinstall its recorded release with node <ia plugin>/scripts/kit.mjs add --from <release> --apply or to remove .ia/governance.json. Nothing extends the list at run time. Every child the three runners start gets an allowlisted environment (never NODE_OPTIONS or NODE_PATH), its process tree is killed on its deadline, and a hook child keeps at most 256 KiB of stdout and of stderr: hygiene, not a sandbox."
  work
    title "Trust kit code by publisher and recorded digest"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-31"
    ended "2026-10-31"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which kits may run code through handlers.json, onwrite.json and checks.json, and with what around it?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A kit may run code (a handlers.json run script, an onwrite.json command or a checks.json command) only when its publisher, the kit id's namespace, with which a .ia/release.json publisher field must agree, is on the plugin's config/trusted-publishers.json, which ships as inventarch alone, and its sources digest equals its latest release's recorded sources, with .ia/release.json naming that release; a handler's run script must also be a declared asset, except the core's own allowlisted ${PLUGIN} script (decision public-method-advisory-run-points), whose sidecar the digest covers. Any other kit, and a kit with no recorded release, is data only: a handler entry may carry a context text, delivered as additionalContext with no process; its run entries and onwrite.json commands are skipped, its checks are refused with IA-CORE-KIT-UNTRUSTED, and the SessionStart answer names each such kit once, with why. A pre handler of a kit whose publisher is not on the list never blocks. In a governed workspace a trusted pre handler that fails, times out or answers past the output cap refuses the call, and an altered gate refuses every pre-tool call: while a kit whose id names a trusted publisher declares a pre handler whose code the trust check refuses (its bytes are not its recorded release, or its script is not a declared asset), each such call is refused, naming the kit and the fix, which is to reinstall its recorded release with node <ia plugin>/scripts/kit.mjs add --from <release> --apply or to remove .ia/governance.json. Nothing extends the list at run time. Every child the three runners start gets an allowlisted environment (never NODE_OPTIONS or NODE_PATH), its process tree is killed on its deadline, and a hook child keeps at most 256 KiB of stdout and of stderr: hygiene, not a sandbox."
    rationale "An adversarial review of making runtime/local's restricted worker a sandbox for kit handlers found that Node's permission model leaves the network open, lets child processes run unconfined and lets a debugger attach to another of the person's Node processes, so no handler sandbox exists to approve. Trust therefore rests on who published a kit and whether its bytes are the release they recorded, the same for every runner, and until the execution-binding release only InventArch-published kits run code. The list is not extensible at run time, because an agent with a shell can write any setting, environment variable or file in the IA home and so could approve itself. The publisher is a claim, not a signature, and the digest is checked against the kit's own release record and covers its declared entry scripts, not every module they import: this stops drift and other publishers' kits, not a deliberate impersonation, which signed releases will close. An altered gate fails closed (operator ruling, 2026-10-31): a governance kit whose bytes drifted must not leave a governed repository silently ungoverned, and a kit from a publisher that is not on the list cannot block, so installing one never stops a person's work."

@decision public-kits-package
  meaning
    says "@inventarch/public-kits carries the kits the core bundles as one npm package, generated in packages/public-kits/ by node tools/kit.mjs package from plugins/ia/config/kits and kits/index.json. Its committed package.json holds its own semver, raised whenever a carried release changes (consistency --base checks it), and ia.kits: each kit's id, latest version, IA archive and manifest sha256, sources digest and folder; it has no scripts, dependencies, entry points or exports. The built files, gitignored, are archives/<archive>.ia.tgz (each recorded release packed again with the pinned CLI, refused unless it is that release) with archives/<archive>.distribution.json (its manifest), kits/<folder>/ byte for byte, index.json, LICENSE and NOTICE; the kit folders stay because an archive carries no sidecars. package --check is a gate in lefthook, npm run check and the gates workflow. .github/workflows/publish-public-kits.yml, started only by workflow_dispatch from main, checks the package against its sources, refuses a version npm already has, builds it and runs npm publish --provenance --access public under OIDC trusted publishing with no token; running it is the operator's step."
  work
    title "Publish the bundled kits as @inventarch/public-kits"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-10-31"
    ended "2026-10-31"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How do the default kits reach a door or workspace that has no plugin, and how is that package built and published?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "@inventarch/public-kits carries the kits the core bundles as one npm package, generated in packages/public-kits/ by node tools/kit.mjs package from plugins/ia/config/kits and kits/index.json. Its committed package.json holds its own semver, raised whenever a carried release changes (consistency --base checks it), and ia.kits: each kit's id, latest version, IA archive and manifest sha256, sources digest and folder; it has no scripts, dependencies, entry points or exports. The built files, gitignored, are archives/<archive>.ia.tgz (each recorded release packed again with the pinned CLI, refused unless it is that release) with archives/<archive>.distribution.json (its manifest), kits/<folder>/ byte for byte, index.json, LICENSE and NOTICE; the kit folders stay because an archive carries no sidecars. package --check is a gate in lefthook, npm run check and the gates workflow. .github/workflows/publish-public-kits.yml, started only by workflow_dispatch from main, checks the package against its sources, refuses a version npm already has, builds it and runs npm publish --provenance --access public under OIDC trusted publishing with no token; running it is the operator's step."
    rationale "The operator's rulings rereview-ia-q1-default-kits-package, dec-122 and dec-133 (C7, C7b) send the default kits to a bare door as one npm package that a lock records as one entry, with npm as transport only: the IA engine expands each kit from its archive, so the package must carry the archives and their manifests, by the digests their releases recorded. Generating it from the bundled kits keeps it from drifting, and committing only package.json keeps the claim reviewable without a third copy of every kit's bytes in history."

@decision public-cli-3-5-0
