@decision public-upkeep-agent
  meaning
    says "The core ships the upkeep agent (plugins/ia/agents/upkeep.md, background, isolated in a worktree, model sonnet, a tools allowlist) with its deterministic half scripts/upkeep.mjs. upkeep.mjs stale compares content digests of the inputs a kit's derived.json sidecar declares with the digests recorded in .ia/derived.lock.json, and lists records whose source, cites or grounded-by lines name a changed file; it exits 3 and starts nothing when nothing is stale. The agent acts only on that report and returns a patch from its own worktree: it never edits the caller's tree, commits or pushes, and never overwrites an output a person edited. Limits: staleness is by digest, not meaning; build runs a script the sidecar names only with --allow-code and without the kit trust check handlers take; nothing starts the agent automatically."
  work
    title "Ship the upkeep agent"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Does the core ship the upkeep agent, and what may it do?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "The core ships the upkeep agent (plugins/ia/agents/upkeep.md, background, isolated in a worktree, model sonnet, a tools allowlist) with its deterministic half scripts/upkeep.mjs. upkeep.mjs stale compares content digests of the inputs a kit's derived.json sidecar declares with the digests recorded in .ia/derived.lock.json, and lists records whose source, cites or grounded-by lines name a changed file; it exits 3 and starts nothing when nothing is stale. The agent acts only on that report and returns a patch from its own worktree: it never edits the caller's tree, commits or pushes, and never overwrites an output a person edited. Limits: staleness is by digest, not meaning; build runs a script the sidecar names only with --allow-code and without the kit trust check handlers take; nothing starts the agent automatically."
    rationale "Derived outputs and the records that cite changed files drift silently. A digest check costs no model and decides whether an agent is worth starting; confining the agent to a worktree patch keeps it a proposer."

@decision public-door-codex
  meaning
    says "The Codex manifest of the core names the IA door: mcpServers ./.codex-mcp.json, a file holding server ia run as node ./scripts/door.mjs with cwd ./ (Codex takes a contained ./ command path and cwd, and does not expand CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT, so it cannot use the Claude .mcp.json). The launcher is the same scripts/door.mjs, which starts npx -y @inventarch/mcp-door at the plugin's IA pin and sets IA_KITS_DIR itself. tools/validate.py holds the file to exactly that; tools/smoke-codex.mjs starts the door through Codex (mcpServerStatus/list) and requires ia_session, ia_catalogue and ia_method. Kit handler hooks stay off in Codex (task codex-door-and-handlers)."
  work
    title "Ship the IA door to Codex"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the core's IA door reach Codex?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "The Codex manifest of the core names the IA door: mcpServers ./.codex-mcp.json, a file holding server ia run as node ./scripts/door.mjs with cwd ./ (Codex takes a contained ./ command path and cwd, and does not expand CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT, so it cannot use the Claude .mcp.json). The launcher is the same scripts/door.mjs, which starts npx -y @inventarch/mcp-door at the plugin's IA pin and sets IA_KITS_DIR itself. tools/validate.py holds the file to exactly that; tools/smoke-codex.mjs starts the door through Codex (mcpServerStatus/list) and requires ia_session, ia_catalogue and ia_method. Kit handler hooks stay off in Codex (task codex-door-and-handlers)."
    rationale "Codex users get the same method packets through the door the Claude plugin ships, without hand-writing a config entry; a separate file keeps the Claude door file exact."

@decision public-release-notes
  meaning
    says "A pull request that changes a plugin's shipped files adds a release note, releases/pending/<id>.json (format inventarch-marketplace.release-note.v1: bump patch, minor, major or none, a title, a summary and the plugins it names), with node tools/release.mjs note, and never bumps the plugin by hand; consistency --base refuses a shipped change unless the plugin's version rose or a note new or changed since the base names it, and release.mjs check, a gate, refuses a malformed note. The release pull request workflow, started only by an operator's dispatch from main, runs release.mjs version --write (the highest bump among each plugin's notes; both manifests, sources.lock.json pluginVersion and the CHANGELOG entry; releases/changesets/<plugin>-<version>.json; the consumed notes removed) and selection.py --write with GITHUB_TOKEN and no dependency install, force-pushes release/next, opens its pull request as a draft or updates the existing pull request without changing its readiness, and dispatches Gates on it. Merging it with a merge commit starts the release workflow, which waits for Gates on that main commit and then creates the <plugin>-v<version> tag and GitHub release. Kit releases keep their own two-commit flow, and publishing @inventarch/public-kits stays its own dispatch."
  work
    title "Release plugins from pending notes through an operator-started release pull request"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does a change to a plugin reach a released version, and who starts a release?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A pull request that changes a plugin's shipped files adds a release note, releases/pending/<id>.json (format inventarch-marketplace.release-note.v1: bump patch, minor, major or none, a title, a summary and the plugins it names), with node tools/release.mjs note, and never bumps the plugin by hand; consistency --base refuses a shipped change unless the plugin's version rose or a note new or changed since the base names it, and release.mjs check, a gate, refuses a malformed note. The release pull request workflow, started only by an operator's dispatch from main, runs release.mjs version --write (the highest bump among each plugin's notes; both manifests, sources.lock.json pluginVersion and the CHANGELOG entry; releases/changesets/<plugin>-<version>.json; the consumed notes removed) and selection.py --write with GITHUB_TOKEN and no dependency install, force-pushes release/next, opens its pull request as a draft or updates the existing pull request without changing its readiness, and dispatches Gates on it. Merging it with a merge commit starts the release workflow, which waits for Gates on that main commit and then creates the <plugin>-v<version> tag and GitHub release. Kit releases keep their own two-commit flow, and publishing @inventarch/public-kits stays its own dispatch."
    rationale "The operator asked for the public repository to release the way inventarch/ia does, with changesets (2026-11-01). Bumping in every pull request made parallel pull requests collide on the version and let a merged change ship unreleased when its bump was forgotten, as the upkeep spike did; a note per change and one release pull request remove both. The release starts only on dispatch, as the inventarch/ia ruling on release automation chose, and the push to release/next and the release pull request stay on the workflow's own token until the dedicated release credential of issue 3 lands. A note pending at the base does not count for a later change, so every shipped change is described by the pull request that made it."

@decision public-main-rulesets
  meaning
    says "Three repository rulesets, kept as JSON under .github/rulesets/ and applied through the GitHub API. main (no bypass) on the default branch: no deletion, no force push, changes only through a pull request with every review thread resolved, stale approvals dismissed, merge commits as the only merge method, and the Gates (ubuntu-latest) and Native verified (ubuntu-latest) checks passing on a branch up to date with main. main-review: a code owner's review (.github/CODEOWNERS), with the operator's account as the only bypass, in pull_request mode, so the operator's and the operator's agents' own pull requests are never blocked. tags (no bypass): no tag may be deleted, moved or force-updated. The repository allows merge commits only, and its default workflow token is read-only."
  work
    title "Protect main and the tags with rulesets"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What protects main and the release tags now that the repository is public?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "Three repository rulesets, kept as JSON under .github/rulesets/ and applied through the GitHub API. main (no bypass) on the default branch: no deletion, no force push, changes only through a pull request with every review thread resolved, stale approvals dismissed, merge commits as the only merge method, and the Gates (ubuntu-latest) and Native verified (ubuntu-latest) checks passing on a branch up to date with main. main-review: a code owner's review (.github/CODEOWNERS), with the operator's account as the only bypass, in pull_request mode, so the operator's and the operator's agents' own pull requests are never blocked. tags (no bypass): no tag may be deleted, moved or force-updated. The repository allows merge commits only, and its default workflow token is read-only."
    rationale "The repository went public on 2026-11-01 with main unprotected, and a pull request had already merged with red checks. GitHub never counts an author's own approval and nearly every pull request here is authored by the operator's account, so a code-owner rule without a bypass would block the operator; splitting the review rule into its own ruleset gives that one account a pull_request bypass while CI, the pull request itself and the history rules bind everyone. Merge commits only, because a kit release pins its sources commit and a squash or rebase would rewrite it. A tag, once the release workflow makes it, is what a user can pin, so nothing may move it."

@decision public-account-connector
  meaning
    says "The core ships two MCP servers, both in plugins/ia/.mcp.json: the IA door, server ia (public-door-mcp), and the InventArch account connector, server inventarch: type http, url https://api.inventarch.dev/mcp, oauth.clientId claude-code, oauth.callbackPort 43822 (the pre-registered public Claude Code client of the API's remote MCP, PKCE S256, callbacks http://localhost:43822/callback and http://127.0.0.1:43822/callback). The plugin ships no secret and stores no token; the host runs the OAuth sign-in (/mcp or claude mcp login inventarch). Claude Code names its tools mcp__plugin_ia_inventarch__<tool> (kit_offerings, kit_download). ia:init section 6 uses them after sign-in: it lists the offerings, downloads each licensed kit through scripts/kit-fetch.mjs (the signed URL goes in on stdin, is never printed or stored, and the archive is written only when its SHA-256 equals the offered digest), installs it with kit.mjs add --from and binds as the kit recommends. The Codex manifest is unchanged: Codex still names only the door (.codex-mcp.json), because this catalogue has not qualified an HTTP server with a pre-registered OAuth client there (task codex-account-connector). tools/validate.py admits that pair, exactly, and nothing else."
  work
    title "Bundle the InventArch account connector with the core"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which MCP servers does the core ship, and how does an account connect?"
    decider "Thomas"
    choice "The core ships two MCP servers, both in plugins/ia/.mcp.json: the IA door, server ia (public-door-mcp), and the InventArch account connector, server inventarch: type http, url https://api.inventarch.dev/mcp, oauth.clientId claude-code, oauth.callbackPort 43822 (the pre-registered public Claude Code client of the API's remote MCP, PKCE S256, callbacks http://localhost:43822/callback and http://127.0.0.1:43822/callback). The plugin ships no secret and stores no token; the host runs the OAuth sign-in (/mcp or claude mcp login inventarch). Claude Code names its tools mcp__plugin_ia_inventarch__<tool> (kit_offerings, kit_download). ia:init section 6 uses them after sign-in: it lists the offerings, downloads each licensed kit through scripts/kit-fetch.mjs (the signed URL goes in on stdin, is never printed or stored, and the archive is written only when its SHA-256 equals the offered digest), installs it with kit.mjs add --from and binds as the kit recommends. The Codex manifest is unchanged: Codex still names only the door (.codex-mcp.json), because this catalogue has not qualified an HTTP server with a pre-registered OAuth client there (task codex-account-connector). tools/validate.py admits that pair, exactly, and nothing else."
    rationale "Nobody should hand-run claude mcp add, an admin CLI or an environment edit: signing in once should be enough to set everything up. The API has one pre-registered public client for Claude Code and no dynamic client registration, and Claude Code accepts that client in a plugin's .mcp.json (claude plugin validate passes it), so the plugin can carry the whole connection without a secret. This supersedes public-door-mcp, which made the door the only MCP server. Options considered: (1) Keep the single IA door and tell the user to run claude mcp add by hand; (2) Bundle the account connector with the Claude Code OAuth client in the core; (3) Ship an installer script that registers the server and the client for the user. Chosen: (2)."
  relationships
    supersedes @decision public-door-mcp

@decision public-account-pointer
  meaning
    says "At session startup only, once per session, never blocking, in one line, the core points the user to sign in to InventArch through the bundled connector: run /mcp and choose inventarch (or claude mcp login inventarch). It makes no network call, reads no credentials and writes nothing. An account counts as present when a design binding is set in the environment or the host lists an inventarch MCP server the user added by hand in its documented local configuration. Limits: a sign-in to the bundled connector is kept with the host's credentials, which the core never opens, so after a sign-in the pointer still shows once at startup, says to ignore it when already signed in, and never blocks. It is suppressed by 'accountOffer': false in ~/.ia/config.json, by IA_NO_ACCOUNT_OFFER=1, or by a non-empty CI."
  work
    title "Point at the connector sign-in instead of teaching claude mcp add"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-01"
    ended "2026-11-01"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "What does the session-start account offer say now that the core bundles the connector?"
    decider "Thomas"
    choice "At session startup only, once per session, never blocking, in one line, the core points the user to sign in to InventArch through the bundled connector: run /mcp and choose inventarch (or claude mcp login inventarch). It makes no network call, reads no credentials and writes nothing. An account counts as present when a design binding is set in the environment or the host lists an inventarch MCP server the user added by hand in its documented local configuration. Limits: a sign-in to the bundled connector is kept with the host's credentials, which the core never opens, so after a sign-in the pointer still shows once at startup, says to ignore it when already signed in, and never blocks. It is suppressed by 'accountOffer': false in ~/.ia/config.json, by IA_NO_ACCOUNT_OFFER=1, or by a non-empty CI."
    rationale "With the connector bundled, the offer no longer teaches a command; it points at the sign-in. Calling the API to learn whether the user is signed in would break the no-network rule and would not help a first-time user; reading the host's credentials file would break the public-core-hooks promise that the hook reads no credentials, so a sign-in through the connector is not detected and the pointer stays one line that may be ignored. This supersedes public-account-offer. Options considered: (1) Keep the single IA door and tell the user to run claude mcp add by hand; (2) Point at the sign-in the bundled connector already offers; (3) Detect the sign-in by calling the API. Chosen: (2)."
  relationships
    supersedes @decision public-account-offer

# The IA 3.6.0 migration (2026-11-08). Release N migrates on the 3.5.x pins; release N+1 moves to 3.6.0.

@decision public-cli-3-6-0
  meaning
    says "Exactly @inventarch/cli 3.6.0 and @inventarch/mcp-door 3.6.0 from release N+1 (language base inventarch/language 3.2.0, archive 7b36e4b4, unchanged from 3.5.0); every kit is re-cut with it, and the core's launchers, closure modules and qualified runtime cohort name it. Release N first migrates users onto installed kits on the 3.5.x pins (public-cli-3-6-0-door-pin)."
  work
    title "Pin @inventarch/cli 3.6.0"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which IA CLI do the catalogue and its kits target after the 3.6.0 release?"
    decider "Operator"
    choice "Exactly @inventarch/cli 3.6.0 and @inventarch/mcp-door 3.6.0 from release N+1, after release N migrates users onto installed kits on the 3.5.x pins."
    rationale "One exact CLI keeps every packed archive reproducible. It takes effect with release N+1, when the pins task records that it supersedes public-cli-3-5-0; @inventarch/cli 3.5.0 stays in force through release N."
  relationships
    supersedes @decision public-cli-3-5-0

@decision public-cli-3-6-0-door-pin
  meaning
    says "Two releases. Release N ships the migration (public-cli-3-6-0-bind E, public-cli-3-6-0-install-route B) on the 3.5.x CLI and door pins. Release N+1 moves the CLI, the door and the re-cut kits to 3.6.0."
  work
    title "The IA door pin and the release sequence"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Does the plugin move CLI, door and re-cut kits to 3.6.0 in one release, hold the door at 3.5.1 under a separate pin, or ship the migration on 3.5.x first and move to 3.6.0 in the next release?"
    options ["A one release: CLI, door and re-cut kits move to 3.6.0 together with the migration", "B separate door pin held at 3.5.1 while the CLI moves to 3.6.0", "C migrate first on 3.5.x in release N, move CLI, door and kits to 3.6.0 in release N+1"]
    decider "Operator"
    choice "C migrate first on 3.5.x in release N, move CLI, door and kits to 3.6.0 in release N+1"
    rationale "Two releases let repositories install their kits while the 3.5.x door still serves them. The 3.6.0 door accepts a lock row for a kit at any version, and the @inventarch/public-kits package route works with CLI 3.5.0, so a repository that installs in release N keeps working at release N+1."

@decision public-cli-3-6-0-bind
  meaning
    says "kit.mjs bind refuses IA-CORE-KIT-NOT-INSTALLED and writes nothing when the lock does not install the kit, unless --install is passed. --install, and the refusal's remedy, use public-cli-3-6-0-install-route's route: seed a language-only lock when the workspace has none, then ia install --package @inventarch/public-kits@<v>, and fall back to kit.mjs add only for a kit the package does not carry."
  work
    title "kit.mjs bind when the lock does not install the kit"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "When the repository's lock does not install the kit, does kit.mjs bind install it first, refuse naming kit.mjs add, write the keys with a warning, behave as today, or refuse unless --install is passed?"
    options ["A bind installs the kit first, then writes the keys", "B bind refuses IA-CORE-KIT-NOT-INSTALLED naming kit.mjs add and writes nothing", "C bind writes the keys and returns notInstalled with the add step", "D bind unchanged; only docs and the refusal row change", "E bind installs only with --install, otherwise refuses as B"]
    decider "Operator"
    choice "E bind installs only with --install, otherwise refuses as B"
    rationale "A refusal that names the exact remedy is the least surprising default, and --install keeps a one-command setup for those who ask for it. The remedy uses the package route the docs lead with. A workspace with sources but no lock cannot take that route with the CLI alone, so the plugin seeds a language-only lock first; this was verified on CLI 3.5.0 and 3.6.0."

@decision public-cli-3-6-0-install-route
  meaning
    says "The quick start leads with the @inventarch/public-kits package. The plugin seeds a language-only lock where a workspace has none (ia init for a new one), then runs ia install --package @inventarch/public-kits@<v> --apply --yes, then kit.mjs bind."
  work
    title "The default kit install route"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which install route does the plugin's quick start lead with: the vendored kit.mjs add, the @inventarch/public-kits package, or both with the vendored route first?"
    options ["A vendored kit.mjs add then bind", "B ia init, ia install --package @inventarch/public-kits, then bind", "C both documented, vendored first"]
    decider "Operator"
    choice "B ia init, ia install --package @inventarch/public-kits, then bind"
    rationale "One lock entry carries the three public kits, with no vendored bytes in the repository. The obligations are accepted: the plugin reads package entries, the operator publishes the package in each release before the docs name it, and the plugin seeds the language lock where a workspace has none."

@decision public-cli-3-6-0-own-lock
  meaning
    says "The marketplace keeps .ia/distributions.lock.json ignored; CONTRIBUTING and the setup run kit.mjs add <kit> . --apply for the three kits, and .gitignore gains vendor/ia/."
  work
    title "How this repository installs its own kits"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the marketplace repository satisfy door 3.6.0 for its own bound lanes: commit its lock with vendor/ia, install locally per contributor, commit a public-kits package entry, drop its bindings, or commit the lock without vendor/ia?"
    options ["A commit the lock and vendor/ia; moving it at a release is optional", "B lock stays ignored; contributor setup runs kit.mjs add for the three kits", "C commit a lock with one @inventarch/public-kits package entry", "D remove the marketplace's .ia/bindings.json", "E commit the lock only; vendor/ia is added to .gitignore"]
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "B lock stays ignored; contributor setup runs kit.mjs add for the three kits"
    rationale "It keeps decision public-kits-package (no copy of the kits' bytes in history), and the lanes run the carried kits either way. A committed lock without its store or vendor archives was measured not to open the workspace at all: the 3.6.0 door returns IA-CORE-ROOT-UNAVAILABLE, and ia restore and ia validate refuse. So committing the lock on its own was ruled out."

@decision public-cli-3-6-0-plugin-bump
  meaning
    says "Release N+1, the one that moves to 3.6.0, takes a minor bump. With release N at 1.4.0 (public-cli-3-6-0-release-n-bump B), that is 1.5.0."
  work
    title "The ia plugin version bump for release N+1"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which bump does the ia plugin release that moves to 3.6.0 take: minor (1.5.0) or major (2.0.0)?"
    options ["A minor, 1.5.0", "B major, 2.0.0"]
    decider "Operator"
    choice "A minor, 1.5.0"
    rationale "The new door refuses a kit the repository binds but does not install, and the plugin's session start names the remedy, so the change is guided. Release N is itself a minor (public-cli-3-6-0-release-n-bump), so this release is 1.5.0."

@decision public-cli-3-6-0-kit-level
  meaning
    says "Each kit re-cut in this migration is a patch on that kit's latest release at the time of the stamp. The expected numbers are ia-method 0.7.3, ia-kb 0.9.4 and ia-authoring 0.1.4; if another release of a kit lands first (PR #26 proposes ia-method 0.8.0), the patch follows it (ia-method 0.8.1)."
  work
    title "The level of each kit re-cut"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "At what level are the three kits re-cut on 3.6.0: patch for all, by content, or minor for all?"
    options ["A patch for all three (0.7.3, 0.9.4, 0.1.4)", "B by content: minor where records change meaning, patch otherwise", "C minor for all three"]
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "A patch for all three (0.7.3, 0.9.4, 0.1.4)"
    rationale "A re-cut that only follows the CLI is a patch, as it was at 3.5.0. The install-guidance changes bring the kits into line with IA 3.6.0's rule and add no capability, and the release notes name them. The level was chosen, not the numbers: a release of a kit that lands first moves the patch with it. A kit whose archive is unchanged is not re-cut: in release N ia-method stays at 0.7.2, because only its README changed, which is outside its archive. At 3.6.0 the pinned CLI changes, so all three kits are re-cut on it (consistency requires each latest release to be packed with the pin); ia-method is 0.7.3."

@decision public-cli-3-6-0-handoff
  meaning
    says "Every spawn of a resolved CLI sets IA_NO_HANDOFF=1 in the child environment: runIa, ia.mjs, the ia-authoring kit's scratch.mjs, the closure runtime, method-check and the gate tools."
  work
    title "Guarding the chosen CLI against hand-off"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Where does the plugin set IA_NO_HANDOFF=1: on every spawn of a resolved CLI, only where exact bytes are attested, nowhere but verify the version after resolving, or nowhere?"
    options ["A every spawn of a resolved CLI", "B only the attested paths (closure runtime, packRelease, restore)", "C nowhere; verify ia --version against cli.version and refuse a mismatch", "D nowhere; document"]
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "A every spawn of a resolved CLI"
    rationale "CLI 3.6.0 hands a typed command to the repository's installed CLI unless IA_NO_HANDOFF=1 is set. Setting it wherever the plugin chose a CLI keeps the attested exact-CLI path and the reported cli.version true. The variable has no effect on a 3.5.x CLI."

@decision public-cli-3-6-0-default-workspace
  meaning
    says "When no consumer workspace is named, the closure path takes IA's repository default only when it is one of the admitted workspaces that root a distribution this repository authors, and refuses (unambiguous) otherwise; a repository that authors no distribution takes the default."
  work
    title "The closure path default workspace"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "When no consumer workspace is named, does the closure path adopt IA's repository default, keep refusing when several workspaces root the distribution, or adopt the default only when it is one of the distribution's roots?"
    options ["A adopt IA's repository default and change the test", "B keep refusing when more than one admitted workspace roots the distribution", "C adopt IA's default only when it is a distribution root; refuse otherwise"]
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "C adopt IA's default only when it is a distribution root; refuse otherwise"
    rationale "Since IA 3.5.1 the unique workspace in .ia/src/workspace.ia is the repository default. Taking it only when it is one of the workspaces that root a distribution this repository authors keeps the guarantee that a packaged composition is never chosen by accident, and it agrees with IA whenever the default is one of them."

@decision public-cli-3-6-0-resolver
  meaning
    says "scripts/resolve.mjs and lib/qualified-resolve.mjs refuse IA-CORE-KIT-NOT-INSTALLED with the door's message and error.kit, before retaining or writing anything, as door 3.6.0 does."
  work
    title "The plugin resolver and the lock requirement"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Does the plugin's own resolver refuse a bound kit the lock does not install, as door 3.6.0 does, or stay permissive?"
    options ["A resolve.mjs and qualified-resolve.mjs refuse IA-CORE-KIT-NOT-INSTALLED with the door's message and error.kit, before retaining or writing anything", "B resolve.mjs stays permissive and is documented as differing from the door"]
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "A resolve.mjs and qualified-resolve.mjs refuse IA-CORE-KIT-NOT-INSTALLED with the door's message and error.kit, before retaining or writing anything"
    rationale "The session start sends hosts that lack the door's tools through resolve.mjs. A permissive resolver would serve them packets the 3.6.0 door refuses. This plugin's resolver and checks (resolve.mjs, check.mjs) enforce it from release N."

@decision public-cli-3-6-0-release-n-bump
  meaning
    says "Release N, the migration on 3.5.x, takes a minor bump to 1.4.0, so release N+1, the move to 3.6.0, is 1.5.0."
  work
    title "The ia plugin version bump for release N"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Which bump does release N, the migration on 3.5.x, take: patch (1.3.1, keeping N+1 at 1.4.0) or minor (1.4.0, making N+1 1.5.0)?"
    options ["A patch, 1.3.1", "B minor, 1.4.0 (N+1 becomes 1.5.0)"]
    decider "Operator"
    choice "B minor, 1.4.0 (N+1 becomes 1.5.0)"
    rationale "Release N changes kit.mjs bind, which now refuses without --install, and leads the docs with the package route. That makes it a minor release, 1.4.0, so release N+1 is 1.5.0."

@decision public-kit-lookup-installed
  meaning
    says "The core still finds a kit to read in its own config/kits, then in <IA home>/kits/<kit>/<version>/, and keeps public-kit-lookup's verbs (list, add, bind, upgrade, repin, rebind and check in plugins/ia/scripts/kit.mjs; pack, bundle, index and convert in tools/kit.mjs; every write validates first and previews unless --apply) and its kits/index.json broadcast. A key the repository binds runs only a kit the repository's own .ia/distributions.lock.json installs (a packages row or a package entry): this plugin's resolver and checks (resolve.mjs, check.mjs) enforce it from release N, and the IA door enforces it too."
  work
    title "Run a bound kit only when the repository installs it"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-08"
    ended "2026-11-08"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Where does the core find a kit, and which kit may a bound key run?"
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "The core still finds a kit to read in its own config/kits, then in <IA home>/kits/<kit>/<version>/, and keeps public-kit-lookup's verbs (list, add, bind, upgrade, repin, rebind and check in plugins/ia/scripts/kit.mjs; pack, bundle, index and convert in tools/kit.mjs; every write validates first and previews unless --apply) and its kits/index.json broadcast. A key the repository binds runs only a kit the repository's own .ia/distributions.lock.json installs (a packages row or a package entry): this plugin's resolver and checks (resolve.mjs, check.mjs) enforce it from release N, and the IA door enforces it too."
    rationale "IA 3.6.0's door refuses IA-CORE-KIT-NOT-INSTALLED for a bound kit the lock does not install, so a carried or IA-home copy no longer stands in for a bound key. The plugin's own resolver applies the same rule, so that the two routes agree."
  relationships
    supersedes @decision public-kit-lookup

@decision public-method-report-gate
  meaning
    says "The ia plugin ships two advisory method reports, exit-evidence.mjs and method-gate.mjs, that read a repository's work records against the principle method-claims-within-evidence and the provisional-foundation ceiling; they report and never block."
  work
    title "Ship the method reports as a report-only gate"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-09"
    ended "2026-11-09"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How does the ia plugin report whether closed tasks carry exit evidence and stay within the ceiling, and what does each script's exit code mean?"
    options ["A advisory reports that exit 0 for findings, with exit 2 for usage errors and for a task that reads unknown", "B reports that exit 1 on a finding", "C no report until a blocking step exists"]
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "A exit-evidence.mjs reports each closed task's evidence from the observation records that name it, and method-gate.mjs runs it with ceiling.mjs; both only report, and the rules are named ceiling and exit-evidence. Positions, first match wins: refused (a current refusal), unknown (the task or an observation could not be read: not evaluated), evidenced (a current success), exempt (an observation whose interpretation.basis is nothing-to-run: the author's declaration, not verified, listed and not a finding), inconclusive (a current inconclusive verdict), stale (a success at another revision, or with no revision recorded) and no-evidence. An observation is current when its evidence.subject-revision equals the task's own digest; a refusal at another revision, or with none recorded, is not current and is listed as information. Reading chosen here, which the principle method-claims-within-evidence does not state: a current refusal stands beside an exemption and beside an unreadable observation, and an exemption needs no currency because it is only a declaration. ia next is looser: it calls a task evidenced on one current success whatever else was observed. Exit codes: exit-evidence.mjs exits 0 whenever it produced a report, findings included, and each line starts with the capitalised position, so exit 0 cannot be read as clear; exit 2 means a usage error, an IA refusal, a missing CLI, a task that is not admitted or not a task, or, with --task, a task that reads unknown, which mirrors ceiling.mjs: exit 2 from the standalone script means unknown, not a finding and never clear; it never exits 1 and it refuses --strict. method-gate.mjs maps every unknown or refused result to not evaluated and exits 0, prints the number of rules evaluated, and exits 2 only for bad usage, a --root that is not a directory, and --strict; a nonzero exit waits for a later step that makes the reports blocking. Not shipped in this change: durable check outputs, because their only reader is a later report and keeping them raises questions this change does not settle (up to 64 MiB per stream, possible secrets in the output, a new directory for every run, which run to keep). This catalogue's own CI runs the method gate as a report only, in a separate workflow (public-method-gate-ci); the plugin README shows how a consumer's CI can. nothing-to-run is this catalogue's own basis id, outside the learning-system basis set; it is not to be authored until learning-system admits it."
    rationale "The principle is not enforced by anything yet, so a first report has to be honest about what it did not evaluate and cannot be mistaken for a pass: every line starts with its position, a rule it could not evaluate is printed in capitals, and a run that evaluated nothing says so. A nonzero exit on a finding would turn a report into a block before the reports are trusted on real repositories."

@decision public-method-gate-ci
  meaning
    says "This catalogue runs the ia plugin's method gate on its own work records in a separate, report-only workflow, and carries observation records for the closed tasks its public records support."
  work
    title "Run the method gate on this catalogue's records as a report"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-09"
    ended "2026-11-09"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "Does this catalogue run the method gate on its own records, how, and what evidence do its closed tasks carry?"
    options ["A a separate report-only workflow", "B a step in the Gates workflow", "C no run in this catalogue"]
    decider "The merge of the pull request that adds this record, reviewed by a code owner"
    choice "A a separate workflow, .github/workflows/method-gate.yml, with one Ubuntu job that restores the workspace (npm ci, then npm run kits:install, because a fresh checkout is not an admitted IA workspace), passes every plan the records author by its full identity, and prints method-gate.mjs's report to the job summary. It runs on pull requests into main, on pushes to main and on dispatch, with a read-only token. It exits 0 on findings; a setup failure turns it red; and when the report says a rule or task was NOT EVALUATED or UNKNOWN it raises a warning, because exit 0 alone does not show that everything was checked; method-gate.mjs asks the CLI to validate the workspace first and, unless it is admitted (refused, any other status, or validate cannot run), reads both rules NOT EVALUATED (workspace not admitted) instead of reading every closed task as having no evidence. It is not one of the required checks (Gates (ubuntu-latest) and Native verified (ubuntu-latest) stay as they are), and making it block is later work. The closed tasks carry the evidence the rule reads: 16 of the 18 closed tasks have one observation each in .ia/src/evidence.ia, with origin check or run, verdict success, interpretation.basis inference and the public pull request, CI run, tag or npm record as locator. They were recorded by an AI agent from those public records, not reviewed by a maintainer, and each states its limits; the two host-gate tasks rest on the committed Codex smoke report only, because no public record holds the claude plugin validate --strict output. cli-3-6-0-install-migration and cli-3-6-0-own-lock stay without evidence, because each names a deliverable this repository does not hold (a test of the real doors, and a kept packet). The gate reads 18 closed tasks: 16 evidenced, 0 exempt, 2 no evidence. No task is exempt, because an exemption is only a declaration. Durable evidence bundles are deferred: the observations' bundle and digest fields read not retained, so the language admits the records but the learning-system consumer cannot read them. The workspace also composes learning-system, the owner of the observation word. Adopted by the merge of the pull request that adds this record, which a code owner reviews."
    rationale "A separate file keeps the required Gates job and the tests that pin its steps unchanged, and a job that is not required can be slow or fail without blocking a merge. The catalogue authors the records the gate reads, so running it here shows what a consumer sees, including the case where it checked less than it says. An observation that claimed more than its public record shows would make the gate read clear when it is not, so each states only what its record shows and a task whose deliverable is absent stays a finding."

@decision public-method-advisory-run-points
  meaning
    says "The ia-method kit's onwrite.json and handlers.json run the core's scripts/method-advise.mjs, which reports the ceiling and the exit evidence of the tasks in the .ia records just written or still uncommitted, as advisory output that blocks nothing."
  work
    title "Run the method reports as advisory hooks from the method kit"
    status made
    owner "InventArch maintainers"
    start "2026-11-10"
    ended "2026-11-10"
    source "docs/decisions.md"
  decision
    question "How do the method reports run where the records change, without a kit shipping code and without blocking anything?"
    options ["A advisory hooks that run one allowlisted script of the core", "B a script shipped inside the kit", "C a context reminder only"]
    decider "Maintainers"
    choice "A: the method kit's two sidecars, onwrite.json and handlers.json, run one script of the core, scripts/method-advise.mjs, as advisory hooks that block nothing: after a Write, Edit or MultiEdit of a .ia file (onwrite.json, extension .ia, no marker; this path alone covers file writes, so no handlers.json entry names Write or Edit and no write is advised twice), after a Bash or PowerShell call, and at a resumed or compacted session (handlers.json, tool post and session). The script runs the ceiling and exit-evidence reports for the tasks in the changed or uncommitted .ia records under .ia/src (the one just written, or those still uncommitted; in a tracked file only the records that differ from HEAD, and a removed or changed observation puts the task it was about in scope; a new file whole), at most 2 from a written file and 3 per shell or session call, after asking the CLI to admit the workspace, and prints not evaluated instead of findings when it does not. It states how many tasks it evaluated, how many it did not and why, how many came from the cache, and how many sit in unchanged records it did not examine, and says so when git failed and a file was read whole; it prints a task it could not evaluate as not evaluated and never as clear, and the same for a .ia record inside .ia/src that it refuses to read (over 1 MB, unreadable); it never downloads a CLI (it needs @inventarch/cli installed in the repository, or IA_CLI_PATH); each read gets 5 seconds and the run 18. A small cache under the IA home, in a folder named advice, holds the last result per task: a finding is reported once, again after a resume or compact, and kept an hour; a clean result is kept 10 minutes; a not-evaluated result is said once, again after a resume or compact, and kept 5 minutes, and is tried again at once when a CLI can be found; a different file content, git HEAD, plugin version, kit version or CLI starts over. Handler run form: a handlers.json run of exactly ${PLUGIN}/scripts/method-advise.mjs, an allowlist of literal names in lib/handlers.mjs (PLUGIN_RUNS) and not a pattern, is accepted on session and tool post handlers and refused on tool pre, prompt and stop handlers; it takes no arguments, runs only when that script is the plugin's own file once links are followed, only for a trusted kit (public-kit-trust) and only inside an IA workspace, so a repository with no .ia starts no process. A core that predates the form skips such a kit with a reason. Not shipped: any blocking, a pre, stop or prompt handler, a whole-plan run in a hook (scripts/method-gate.mjs is that report), judgement-based assessment and durable receipts. A closed task that was committed with no evidence is not reported by a hook, and a change to a spec or decision does not re-check the tasks that cite it; a removed record is covered only when it was the subject of an observation, which puts the task it was about in scope. The full report is the only complete check, and a hook's silence is not a clear result."
    rationale "The exit-evidence and ceiling rules were only reports an agent had to remember to run; running them where the records change puts the finding next to the work, while a hook budget of seconds rules out the whole plan, so the scope is the records that changed and is always stated. The kit ships no script, because a fetched release carries no executables and the reports already live in the core; an allowlist of one literal name keeps a trusted kit from running any other script of the core. A skipped check must not stand for a pass, so every case that could not run is printed, and nothing here blocks."
